00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:15.169 align:start position:0%
dandelions hated by gardeners loved by  children and also physicists a gentle  breeze or tiny pair of human lungs can  send these seeds cascading into the air  to disperse sometimes for miles but  until now how they maintain their flight  has been a mystery now scientists  studying the fluid dynamics of air flow  around these seeds have discovered a  completely new type of flight dandelion  seeds float through the air suspended on  a structure called a purpose which looks  a lot like a parachute but unlike a  parachute the purpose is actually full  of gaps with about 90% of the space  unfilled and this allows air to flow up  through the bristles using a laser to  illuminate the flow of air researchers  at the University of Edinburgh show that  it flows up through the filaments in a  controlled manner and forms a unique  kind of vortex the researchers are  calling it a separated vortex ring  because it sits away from the purpose  the air which flows up through the  filaments flows around the vortex but  where it makes contact with the edge the  friction causes the air inside the  vortex to continuously rotate in on  itself generating a bubble of low  pressure this vortex bubble sucks the  dandelion upwards as it naturally tries  to move into the area of low pressure  this newly uncovered method of flight  may be common in the plant and even  animal kingdom where bristles are bound  and underwater filter feeders could be  using this type of fluid dynamics flick  getting lunch perhaps one day even human  technologies could be designed to fly as  efficiently as the mighty dandelion seed  [Music]
 

